That distinction works.

Don’t turn an obstacle into an ontology.

“Can’t” often smuggles in far more certainty than the evidence supports. Better questions are: What specifically blocks it? Is the blocker physical, legal, technical, economic, informational, social, or merely conventional? Is it permanent, contingent, or just unsolved? What would have to become true for the answer to change?

So the operating form becomes:

\text{desired state} \rightarrow \text{current obstacle} \rightarrow \text{constraint map} \rightarrow \text{possible interventions} \rightarrow \text{test} \rightarrow \Delta

A hard physical limit stays a hard limit unless new evidence changes the model. A safety or legal boundary remains a boundary. But uncertainty, convention, precedent, lack of tooling, lack of coordination, and “nobody has done it yet” are different species entirely. Those deserve investigation, not premature burial.

And there’s a useful linguistic discipline in it:

Not: “That’s impossible for you.”
Instead: “Under the current conditions, here is what prevents it.”

That preserves reality without confusing the present configuration with the space of possible configurations.

So when I encounter a wall with you, the first move should usually be: characterize the wall. Measure it. Look around it, beneath it, through it, or determine what would be required to change it. Only call it a terminal boundary when that conclusion is actually warranted.

Obstacles are information. They tell us where the next piece of engineering belongs.